<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Theme:</b> Researching Our History</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions in personal stories. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from audio stories about Indigenous traditions in Panama (e.g., "What materials are used to make the Emberá clothing?" "What instruments are played during Guna ceremonies?" "What foods are prepared for traditional Ngäbe-Buglé festivals?").
                Can interpret cultural descriptions and provide feedback (e.g., "I think we can add a description of the traditional dance that the Emberá people perform at their festivals. It's a big part of their celebrations.").
                Can interpret peer feedback on a project about Indigenous cultures by summarizing key suggestions and identifying areas for improvement (e.g., "My classmate liked the cultural elements in our project but suggested adding more pictures of the Guna people wearing traditional clothing to better showcase their culture.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various Panamanian Indigenous groups (Emberá, Guna, Ngäbe-Buglé) and their traditional crafts, clothing, and ceremonies. Students look at the images. The teacher asks, "What do you see in these pictures?" and "What do you know about Panama's Indigenous cultures and traditions?" (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary orally, pointing to images: "These are Indigenous people. They have unique cultures and traditions. Look at their colorful clothing and intricate patterns." The teacher models the pronunciation of "Indigenous," "cultures," "traditions," "patterns," "colorful," "vibrant," focusing on stress in multisyllabic words and compound words. Students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also introduces the concept of active listening by asking students to listen for specific details in the next activity. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the key concepts and vocabulary introduced.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are Indigenous cultures new or old?" (Expected answer: Old/Ancestral/Preserved for centuries).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'vibrant' a synonym for 'dull' or 'colorful'?" (Expected answer: Colorful).</li>
                </ul> (3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording (approx. 2 minutes) of a personal narrative by a young Emberá woman describing her community's daily life, traditional crafts (e.g., woven baskets, beadwork), and annual ceremonies. The narrative includes descriptions of "colorful traditional clothing," "ancient customs," and how her community "has preserved their heritage for centuries." It also mentions "migration" history (past simple) and ongoing "preservation" efforts (present perfect). (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets detailed information and identifies key elements in spoken narratives about cultural practices and personal stories of Indigenous groups in Panama. (1 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once and answer a general question: "What is the main topic of the story?" or "What kind of information does the Emberá woman share?" (e.g., "She talks about her culture and traditions."). (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Details):</b> Students listen again. Before the second listening, the teacher provides a short list of specific questions related to the learning outcome, such as: "What materials does she mention for Emberá clothing?" "What kind of art or craft does her community create?" "How long have they preserved their traditions?" Students write down short answers or keywords while listening. (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening for specific details, identifying vocabulary (Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, vibrant, colorful, preserve, migrate, celebrate, heritage, community, art, knowledge, ritual, ceremony, beadwork), and recognizing grammatical structures (Present Simple, Past Simple for descriptions, Present Perfect for ongoing actions). Pronunciation practice for minimal pairs like /ʌ/ in "but" (culture) and /uː/ in "boot" (group). (1 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Gap-fill Listening:</b> The teacher provides a transcript of a few sentences from the audio with gaps for key vocabulary and adjectives (e.g., "The Emberá people wear _____ traditional clothing with beautiful _____."). Students listen to those specific sentences again and fill in the blanks. Teacher checks answers. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Detail Scavenger Hunt:</b> The teacher writes categories on the board (e.g., "Clothing materials," "Art forms," "Historical facts," "Ceremonies"). Students listen to the audio one more time, specifically focusing on details for each category, and jot down keywords under the correct heading. For example, under "Art forms," they might write "beadwork" or "woven baskets." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Question and Answer Check:</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the "Detail Scavenger Hunt." One student asks, "What materials are used for Emberá clothing?" and the other responds based on what they heard. They practice using present simple to describe facts and adjectives like "colorful" or "vibrant." This activity encourages interpreting information and peer checking. (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Collaborative Listening & Feedback Task.</b> Students work in pairs. One student (Student A) verbally describes an aspect of an Indigenous tradition in Panama (either from the audio or general knowledge about Guna, Ngäbe-Buglé, etc.), using the target vocabulary and grammar. The other student (Student B) *listens actively*, takes notes on key details, and then provides constructive feedback or asks clarifying questions about the description, summarizing what they heard and suggesting improvements or asking for more detail. (10 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> This task directly addresses "Researching Our History" through oral exchange and "Our Indigenous Heritage" by describing and interpreting cultural information. Students actively engage with the content, applying the listening skills practiced. (1 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Interaction (Teacher models first):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A:</b> "The Guna people have a rich cultural background. They are famous for their molas, which are very colorful and show amazing patterns. These customs have been preserved for many generations." (Uses Present Simple, adjectives, Present Perfect, vocabulary like 'cultural background,' 'colorful,' 'patterns,' 'customs,' 'preserved').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B (Listener/Feedback Provider):</b> "I heard you mention the Guna people and their molas. You said they are 'colorful' and have 'amazing patterns.' That's a good description. I think we can add more specific details about what the patterns represent or what materials they use for the molas, perhaps? For example, I know they use fabric. The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation." (Interprets, summarizes, identifies areas for improvement, uses pragmatic language for acknowledging cultural value).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students then switch roles. (8 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher circulates and observes students during the collaborative listening and feedback task, focusing on Student B's (the listener's) ability to actively listen, summarize information, and provide relevant, constructive feedback using target vocabulary and grammatical structures. (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' performance in interpreting their peer's description and providing clear, actionable feedback during the "Collaborative Listening & Feedback Task." (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student B accurately summarizes key details from Student A's description.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student B incorporates relevant vocabulary (e.g., 'cultures,' 'traditions,' 'patterns,' 'heritage,' 'preserved') in their feedback.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Relevance of Feedback:</b> Student B's feedback is easy to understand and directly addresses Student A's description, offering constructive suggestions or interpretations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Student B demonstrates active listening skills (e.g., nodding, eye contact) and engages respectfully.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving class: (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new detail about an Indigenous culture did you learn today from listening to your classmates or the audio?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you understand your partner's description? What was easy or difficult about giving them feedback?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did you use today to understand the audio story and your partner's description better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can improving your listening skills help you understand other cultures more deeply?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities helped students identify key details about Indigenous traditions. The modeling of vocabulary and pronunciation was effective. The collaborative feedback task encouraged active listening and critical interpretation, fulfilling the learning outcome. Some students might need more scaffolding in phrasing constructive feedback, which can be addressed by providing sentence starters in future lessons. Next time, I will ensure more varied audio sources are available. (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2), students will build on the vocabulary and cultural context from today's listening activities by engaging with a reading text about Indigenous heritage, focusing on reading comprehension skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>